\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Teaching Modules for Audio Sonification, Online 4-Channel ERP Averaging, Frame-Jitter Transfer, and Periodic/Aperiodic Spectra.} \textbf  {(A)} Audio waveforms comparing a relaxed high-alpha state ($650\text  { Hz}$ smooth tone) against an active high-beta/theta state (\texttt  {brainSynth.js}). \textbf  {(B)} Corresponding audio frequency spectra showing a single dominant frequency during relaxation versus harmonic sidebands during active engagement (\texttt  {p5.FFT}). \textbf  {(C)} Online 256~Hz ERP trial averaging ($-200\text  { to }+800\text  { ms}$, positive-up voltage convention in $\mu \text  {V}$, baseline-corrected over $-200\text  { to }0\text  { ms}$; \citealp  {picton2000guidelines,keil2014committee}) across $K = 1, 5,$ and $20$ trials (\texttt  {epochs.js}). Across 1,000 Monte Carlo runs, pre-stimulus baseline RMS noise scales by $1/\sqrt  {K}$ ($13.8\rightarrow 6.1\rightarrow 3.1\ \mu \text  {V}$); in the single realization shown, baseline RMS drops from $17.4\ \mu \text  {V}$ ($\text  {SNR} = 0.55$) to $5.7\ \mu \text  {V}$ ($\text  {SNR} = 1.66$) and $2.7\ \mu \text  {V}$ ($\text  {SNR} = 3.48$), while P300 Standardized Measurement Error ($\text  {SME} = \text  {SD}_{\text  {trials}}/\sqrt  {K}$; \citealp  {luck2021standardized}) drops from $8.7\rightarrow 3.6\rightarrow 1.9\ \mu \text  {V}$. Rejecting a single $115\ \mu \text  {V}$ blink trial ($166.2\ \mu \text  {V}$ peak-to-peak) eliminates $+6.7\ \mu \text  {V}$ of P300 peak bias ($\text  {RMSE}: 4.52\rightarrow 3.33\ \mu \text  {V}$, vs.\ $3.04\ \mu \text  {V}$ blink-free). \textbf  {(D)} Exponential moving-average smoothing and dual-threshold hysteresis used in student neurofeedback games to prevent rapid on/off flicker ($98.4\%$ jitter variance reduction). \textbf  {(E)} Frequency-domain $\text  {sinc}$ transfer function $|H(f)| = |\text  {sinc}(fW)|$ for browser/Bluetooth trigger jitter of $W = 16.7\text  { ms}$ (1 frame @ 60~FPS, $f_c = 26.6\text  { Hz}$), $30\text  { ms}$ ($f_c = 14.8\text  { Hz}$, null at $33.3\text  { Hz}$), and $45\text  { ms}$ ($f_c = 9.8\text  { Hz}$, null at $22.2\text  { Hz}$) overlaid on normalized spectral profiles of the P300 ($\sigma = 58\text  { ms}$), N170 ($\sigma = 26\text  { ms}$), and a narrow $20\text  { ms}$ FWHM peak; across 1,000 20-trial runs under $30\text  { ms}$ jitter, P300 latency SD drops from $31.5\text  { ms}$ (peak picking) to $13.4\text  { ms}$ (dynamic template matching; \citealp  {schubert2026dynamic}) and $10.4\text  { ms}$ (50\% fractional-area latency; \citealp  {schubert2024multiverse}). \textbf  {(F)} Log-log power spectral density ($2\text  {--}40\text  { Hz}$) separating periodic $10\text  { Hz}$ alpha ($+8.5\text  { dB}$ vs.\ $+2.4\text  { dB}$ above the $1/f^{\chi }$ background; $\Delta = 6.2\text  { dB}$ vs.\ $5.4\text  { dB}$ unadjusted) from the aperiodic $1/f^{\chi }$ exponent ($\chi = 1.41$ relaxed vs.\ $1.07$ active; \citealp  {vanbueren2026comparing}).}}{13}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sonification}{{3}{13}{\textbf {Teaching Modules for Audio Sonification, Online 4-Channel ERP Averaging, Frame-Jitter Transfer, and Periodic/Aperiodic Spectra.} \textbf {(A)} Audio waveforms comparing a relaxed high-alpha state ($650\text { Hz}$ smooth tone) against an active high-beta/theta state (\texttt {brainSynth.js}). \textbf {(B)} Corresponding audio frequency spectra showing a single dominant frequency during relaxation versus harmonic sidebands during active engagement (\texttt {p5.FFT}). \textbf {(C)} Online 256~Hz ERP trial averaging ($-200\text { to }+800\text { ms}$, positive-up voltage convention in $\mu \text {V}$, baseline-corrected over $-200\text { to }0\text { ms}$; \citealp {picton2000guidelines,keil2014committee}) across $K = 1, 5,$ and $20$ trials (\texttt {epochs.js}). Across 1,000 Monte Carlo runs, pre-stimulus baseline RMS noise scales by $1/\sqrt {K}$ ($\ERPMCNoiseRMSKOne \rightarrow \ERPMCNoiseRMSKFive \rightarrow \ERPMCNoiseRMSKTwenty \ \mu \text {V}$); in the single realization shown, baseline RMS drops from $\ERPNoiseRMSKOne \ \mu \text {V}$ ($\text {SNR} = \ERPSNRKOne $) to $\ERPNoiseRMSKFive \ \mu \text {V}$ ($\text {SNR} = \ERPSNRKFive $) and $\ERPNoiseRMSKTwenty \ \mu \text {V}$ ($\text {SNR} = \ERPSNRKTwenty $), while P300 Standardized Measurement Error ($\text {SME} = \text {SD}_{\text {trials}}/\sqrt {K}$; \citealp {luck2021standardized}) drops from $\ERPSMEKOne \rightarrow \ERPSMEKFive \rightarrow \ERPSMEKTwenty \ \mu \text {V}$. Rejecting a single $115\ \mu \text {V}$ blink trial ($\ERPBlinkPtPUV \ \mu \text {V}$ peak-to-peak) eliminates $+\ERPBlinkPThreeHundredBiasUV \ \mu \text {V}$ of P300 peak bias ($\text {RMSE}: \ERPRMSEBlinkUnrejectedUV \rightarrow \ERPRMSEBlinkRejectedUV \ \mu \text {V}$, vs.\ $\ERPRMSECleanTwentyUV \ \mu \text {V}$ blink-free). \textbf {(D)} Exponential moving-average smoothing and dual-threshold hysteresis used in student neurofeedback games to prevent rapid on/off flicker ($\EMAVarReductionPct \%$ jitter variance reduction). \textbf {(E)} Frequency-domain $\text {sinc}$ transfer function $|H(f)| = |\text {sinc}(fW)|$ for browser/Bluetooth trigger jitter of $W = 16.7\text { ms}$ (1 frame @ 60~FPS, $f_c = \JitterFCMSixteenHz \text { Hz}$), $30\text { ms}$ ($f_c = \JitterFCThirtyHz \text { Hz}$, null at $\JitterNullThirtyHz \text { Hz}$), and $45\text { ms}$ ($f_c = \JitterFCFortyFiveHz \text { Hz}$, null at $\JitterNullFortyFiveHz \text { Hz}$) overlaid on normalized spectral profiles of the P300 ($\sigma = 58\text { ms}$), N170 ($\sigma = 26\text { ms}$), and a narrow $20\text { ms}$ FWHM peak; across 1,000 20-trial runs under $30\text { ms}$ jitter, P300 latency SD drops from $\JitterPThreePeakLatSDMS \text { ms}$ (peak picking) to $\JitterPThreeTempLatSDMS \text { ms}$ (dynamic template matching; \citealp {schubert2026dynamic}) and $\JitterPThreeAreaLatSDMS \text { ms}$ (50\% fractional-area latency; \citealp {schubert2024multiverse}). \textbf {(F)} Log-log power spectral density ($2\text {--}40\text { Hz}$) separating periodic $10\text { Hz}$ alpha ($+\AlphaPeakAboveOneFRelDB \text { dB}$ vs.\ $+\AlphaPeakAboveOneFActDB \text { dB}$ above the $1/f^{\chi }$ background; $\Delta = \AlphaAdjDiffDB \text { dB}$ vs.\ $\AlphaRawDiffDB \text { dB}$ unadjusted) from the aperiodic $1/f^{\chi }$ exponent ($\chi = \AperiodicExpRelaxed $ relaxed vs.\ $\AperiodicExpActive $ active; \citealp {vanbueren2026comparing})}{figure.caption.5}{}}
